    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 2026 Paris Motor Show: Jeep Reveals Six-Model European Strategy for 2030, Renegade Is Set to Return\nBen Affleck Goes Behind the Camera for Ford's F-150 Raptor Story, Casts Real Engineers\n32 photos\nPhoto: Ford From the Road\nFord Racing has just launched a high-profile ad campaign to mark the 16-year story and raw origin of the Ford F-150 Raptor. Titled \"Project Raptor: Based on a Ford Story,\" the short film is directed by Oscar winner and Raptor owner Ben Affleck and stars fellow actor and Raptor owner (no surprise there!) Jon Bernthal.\nBernthal is actually the narrator alongside the actual engineering masterminds who secretly brought the mighty truck to life. Now, they reveal how the first-ever Ford F-150 Raptor came to life. Back in 2006, a small group of Ford Racing engineers pivoted from street performance to ask a wild question: Could the high-speed capability of a Baja 1000 desert race truck be brought directly to a production vehicle?\nThey already had the answer to that. They knew they could do it. However, they had to face massive internal skepticism. Traditional 4x4s focused on low-range crawling, not flying over sand dunes at high speeds. The project was canceled not once, not twice, but six separate times before it finally got the green light.\nThe \"Raptor\" designation was entirely accidental. It was never intended to be the final production name for the super truck. It started as an internal development codename, but it grew on those involved, and it generated so much excitement that Ford fought for the naming rights.\nPhoto: Ford From the Road When Ford first decided to use it for the production model, they discovered that Mosler Automotive, a boutique American supercar manufacturer, already legally owned the trademark rights to the “Raptor” name. Ford could not legally sell a vehicle under that badge until their legal team negotiated an undisclosed financial settlement with Mosler to purchase the naming rights.\nFurthermore, when Ford tried to expand its truck lineup globally with the Ranger Raptor, they hit a massive roadblock in Australia. Hino Motors, the commercial truck division of Toyota, had owned the Australian trademark for the designation since the early 1990s.\nAnd the fight was far from over. Ford's legal battles for the name did not stop with real cars. They also had to fight over toy cars. When the Jurassic World movie franchise took off, Universal Studios filed a trademark for the term \"Raptor Squad\" to launch a line of toy cars based on the velociraptors in the movie.\nFord opposed Universal in court, arguing that a line of dinosaur-themed toy trucks called \"Raptor Squad\" would directly dilute Ford’s merchandise, confusing buyers who associated the name with the F-150 pickup truck . Universal ultimately agreed to amend their trademark description so it wouldn't conflict with real or toy automotive goods.\nPhoto: Ford From the Road Instead of actors, Ben Affleck chose real engineers for his 90-second fillm. The newly launched commercial puts the spotlight on the Ford Racing engineers who withstood the long sleepless nights and brutal desert testing to roll out a truck that would perform both on and off the road.\nDerek Bier, the Chief Engineer of Ford Racing, literally spent so much time testing the truck in Mexico that he learned Spanish. In the meantime, the Vehicle Architecture and CAE Manager, Tim Smith, was the one who built the truck’s tough skeletal structure. Then, there is Chris Paiva, Vehicle Dynamics Manager, affectionately called \"The Raptor Guy.\" He helped integrate the iconic long-travel FOX Live Valve suspension technology.\nWhat started as a low-profile engineering experiment has grown into a massively popular model and the beginning of a new segment. Sixteen years later, Ford hosted a massive Raptor Rally in Lake Havasu, Arizona, drawing over 1,100 people and 500 Raptor trucks to trail ride and share their experience.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nFord F-150 Raptor Ford Ford Racing Ben Affleck Jon Bernthal\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT Toyota Tundra Goes Single Cab; It's a Short-Bed Sport Truck Now, Though Only Across Imagination Land",
